#def #TODO {useenv center { \bf{\color{red}[Fill Here! (#1)] }}}

Our assertion are nontrivial only if $p\in(1,2)$, when one may encounter
$0^{-1}$ in \cref{eq:2.17}.

Before proving the lemma, we should prove that $u$ is bounded on
$[a,b]\cap\R$.
Since $u\in W_p^2(a,b)$, it is locally absolutely continuous on $[a,b]\cap\R$ and
$u_x\in L_p(a,b)$. Since $p>1$, $|u|^p$ is locally also absolutely continuous and
$(|u|^p)_x = |u|^{p-2}uu_x$.

Hence, for fixed $t_0\in [a,b]\cap\R$, we have
$$
    \sup_{t\in [t_0,b]\cap\R}|u(t)|^p
    <= |u(t_0)|^p + \sup_{t\in [t_0,b]}\int_{t_0}^t |u(r)|^{p-1}|u'(r)|dr
    <= |u(t_0)| + \int_{a}^b |u(r)|^{p-1}|u'(r)|dr.
$$
Now H\"older's inequality shows that
$$
    \int_{a}^b |u(r)|^{p-1}|u'(r)|dr <= ||u||_{L_p(a,b)}^{p-1}||u'||_{L_p(a,b)}.
$$
Such approach is same on $[a,t_0]\cap\R$. Hence, $\sup|u|<oo$.

To prove (i), first assume that $a$ and $b$ are finite,
take a sequence of smooth even positive functions $g_n(t)$ such
that $g_n -> |t|^{p/2-1} \eqcolon g(t)$ in $L_2(-A,A)$ where $A=\sup|u|<oo$.
First of all, although $g$ is undefined at the origin, since $1<p<2$,
$g$ lies in $L_2(-A,A)$.
In addition, it is easy to show that
$$
    F(t) \defeq \int_0^t g(s)ds = {2//p}|t|^{p/2-1}t,\quad
    G(t) \defeq \int_0^t g^2(s)ds = {1//p-1}|t|^{p-2}t.
$$

One can take $g_n(t) = (t^2+n^{-2})^{p/4-1/2}$ for instance.
Indeed, as $1 < p < 2$, we have $0 <= g_n <= g$, and $|g_n - g| <= g$.
Since $g\in L_2(-A,A)$, by the dominated convergence theorem, $||g_n - g||_2 ->
0$ as $n->oo$.

Now define
$$
    F_n(t) \defeq \int_0^t g_n(s)ds\quad G_n(t) \defeq \int_0^t g_n^2(s)ds.
$$
By the H\"older's inequality (here $A<oo$ is used),
$$
    \sup_{t\in[-A,A]} ?|F_n(t) - {2//p}|t|^{p/2-1}t|?
    <= \int_{-A}^A |g_n(s) - g(s)|ds
    <= (2A)^{1/2} ||g_n(s) - g||_{L_2(-A,A)} -> 0;
$$
$$
    \sup_{t\in[-A,A]} ?|G_n(t) - {1//p-1}|t|^{p-2}t|?
    <= \int_{-A}^A |g_n^2(s) - g^2(s)|ds
    <= ||g_n + g||_{L_2(-A,A)}||g_n - g||_{L_2(-A,A)}
    -> 0
$$
as $n->oo$. Next, as $u\in W_p^2(a,b)$, both $u$ and $u_x$ are locally absolutely
continuous. Thus there exists a Lebesgue measurable set $E\subset [a,b]$ such that
$[a,b]-!E$ has measure zero and on $E$,
$$
    (G_n(u)u_x)_x = G_n'(u)(u_x)^2 + G_n(u)u_{xx}.
$$
Taking an integration on both sides, together with the fundamental theorem of
calculus, we obtain
#eq (eq:2.17.1) {
    \int_a^b G_n(u)u_{xx}dx = G_n(u(b))u_x(b) - G_n(u(a))u_x(a) - I_n,
}
where
$$
    I_n = \int_a^b G_n'(u)(u_x)^2dx 
    = \int_a^b g_n^2(u)(u_x)^2dx
    = \int_a^b(v_{nx})^2dx,\quad v_n = F_n(u).
$$

Since $[-A,A]$ is compact and $G_n$ is smooth, $G_n(u)$ is bounded on
$[a,b]$. From this, we have $G_n(u)u_{xx}\in L_1(a,b)$. Since $G_n -> G$
uniformly on $[-A,A]$, the dominated convergence theorem gives that
$G(u)u_{xx}\in L_1(a,b)$.\footnote{
    $||G_nu_xx - Gu_{xx}||_1 <= ||G_n - G||_oo ||u_{xx}||_1 -> 0$ as $n->oo$.
} On the other hand, clearly we have
$$
    \int_a^b v_n^2(x)dx
    = \int_a^b ?( \int_0^{u(x)}g_n(s) ds )?^2 dx
    <= \int_a^b ?( \int_{-A}^{A}g_n(s) ds )?^2 dx
    <= 2A(b-a)||g_n||_{L_2(-A,A)}.
$$

By the dominated convergence theorem we have
useenv align* {
    \limsup_{n->oo}||v_n||_{W_2^1(a,b)}^2
    &<= N\limsup_{n->oo}(||v_n||_{L_2(a,b)}^2 + ||v_{nx}||_{L_2(a,b)}^2) \\
    &<= N\limsup_{n->oo}(||v_n||_{L_2(a,b)}^2 + ||v_{nx}||_{L_2(a,b)}^2) \\
    &<= N\limsup_{n->oo}
    ?(G_n(u(b))u_x(b)-G_n(u(a))u_x(a) - \int_a^b G_n(u)u_{xx}dx )?
    + N\limsup_{n->oo} ||v_{nx}||_{L_2(a,b)}^2 \\
    &<= N
    ?(G(u(b))u_x(b)-G(u(a))u_x(a) - \int_a^b G(u)u_{xx}dx )?
    + NA(b-a)||g||_{L_2(-A,A)} \\
    &< oo.
}
From this, the sequence $v_n$ is bounded in $W_2^1(a,b)$, and thus there exists
a subsequence while using same index and $v\in W_2^1(a,b)$ such that $v_n->v$
weakly in $W_2^1(a,b)$, thus weakly in $L_2(a,b)$.
On the other hand, since $v_n -> F(u)$ uniformly on $[a,b]$, it also converges
in $L_2(a,b)$ by the dominated convergence theorem.
Therefore, $F(u)$ and $v$ are indistinguishable, proving that $F(u) =
(2/p)|u|^{p/2-1}u\in W_2^1(a,b)$.
Its absolute value has the same property, so that $|u|^{p/2}\in W_1^2(a,b)$,
which implies $|u|^{p/2}$ is absolutely continuous.

To go further, it is useful to prove that $v_n->{2//p}|u|^{p/2-1}u$ in
$W_2^1(a,b)$. To this end it suffices to prove that
#eq (eq:2.17.2) {
    \lim_{n->oo}\int_a^b (v_{nx})^2dx = \int_a^b |u|^{p-2}(u_x)^2dx.
}
It follows from \cref{eq:2.17.1} that the limit times $p-1$ equals (by the way,
we use the dominated convergence theorem)
$$
    |u(b)|^{p-2}u(b)u_x(b) - |u(a)|^{p-2}u(a)u_x(a)
    - \int_a^b |u|^{p-2}uu_{xx}dx.
$$
On the other hand let $(a,b)=\bigcup_i(a_i,b_i)$ be a decomposition of $(a,b)$
into disjoint intervals on each of which $u$ has the same sign and vanishes at
end points except perhaps for the cases $a_i=a$ and $b_i=b$.\footnote{
    Recall that every open sets in $\R$ can be expressed as the disjoint union of
    countable open intervals. Now as $u$ is continuous (actually it is
    absolutely continuous), one can consider $\{u > 0\}$ and $\{u < 0\}$,
    respectively.
    However, here, we do not decompose $(a,b)$ itself. Indeed $(a,b)$ is
    connected, but countable disjoint union makes the set non-connected.
    Actually, we decompose a set $(a,b)\cap\{u!=0\}$.
} Then, integrating by parts, we get
#eq (eq:2.17.3) { useenv aligned {
    (p-1)\int_{a_i}^{b_i}|u|^{p-2}(u_x)^2dx
    &= \int_{a_i}^{b_i}u_xd(|u|^{p-2}u) \\
    &= u_x(b_i)|u(b_i)|^{p-2}u(b_i)
    - u_x(a_i)|u(a_i)|^{p-2}u(a_i) - \int_{a_i}^{b_i}|u|^{p-2}uu_{xx}dx.
}}
Indeed, for each interval $(a_j,b_j)$, the sign of $u$ is fixed, say for
instance, $u>=0$ on $(a_j,b_j)$.
Consider any $\epsilon>0$. Then on $(a_j+\epsilon,b_j-\epsilon)$, $|u|^{p-2}$ is
absolutely continuous because there is no point which makes $u = 0$.\footnote{
    Notice that $u(a_j) = 0$ unless $a_j = a$, and $u(b_j) = 0$ unless $b_j =
    b$.
}
by using the integration by parts, one have
useenv align* {
    (p-1)\int_{a_i+\epsilon}^{b_i-\epsilon}|u|^{p-2}(u_x)^2dx
    &= (p-1)\int_{a_i+\epsilon}^{b_i-\epsilon}u^{p-2}(u_x)^2dx \\
    &= \int_{a_i+\epsilon}^{b_i-\epsilon}u_xd(u^{p-1}) \\
    &= u_x(b_i-\epsilon)u(b_i-\epsilon)^{p-1}
    - u_x(a_i+\epsilon)u(a_i+\epsilon)^{p-1} -
      \int_{a_i+\epsilon}^{b_i-\epsilon} u^{p-1} d(u_x) \\
    &= u_x(b_i-\epsilon)u(b_i-\epsilon)^{p-1}
    - u_x(a_i+\epsilon)u(a_i+\epsilon)^{p-1} -
      \int_{a_i+\epsilon}^{b_i-\epsilon} u^{p-1}u_{xx} dx \\
    &= u_x(b_i-\epsilon)|u(b_i-\epsilon)|^{p-2}u(b_i-\epsilon) \\
    &\kern1cm- u_x(a_i+\epsilon)|u(a_i+\epsilon)|^{p-2}u(a_i+\epsilon)
    - \int_{a_i+\epsilon}^{b_i-\epsilon} |u|^{p-2}uu_{xx} dx.
}
Since $u\in W_2^1(a,b)$, at least both $u$ and $u_x$ are continuous.
Thus letting $\epsilon\downarrow 0$ gives \cref{eq:2.17.3}.
Similarly, one can obtain \cref{eq:2.17.3} when $u <= 0$.
Here, notice that our case is not a telescoping series because there is no way
to reorder $a_j$, and $b_j$. However recall that $u(a_j) = 0$ unless $a_j=a$,
and $u(b_j)=0$ unless $b_j=b$. That means, when summing up \cref{eq:2.17.3},
all terms except one before integral in the right hand side is zero, and thus
summing up \cref{eq:2.17.3} gives \cref{eq:2.17.2}.

Now notice that $(G_n(u)u_x)_x = (v_{nx})^2+G_n(u)u_{xx}$, where, by the above
conclusions, the right-hand side converges in $L_1(a,b)$.\footnote{
    Actually, this can obtained by using specific $g_n$, for instance,
    $g_n\uparrow |t|^{p/2-1}$.
} It follows that the limit of $G_n(x)u_x$ is absolutely continuous. The limit
being ${1//p-1}|u|^{p-2}uu_x$, the lemma is proved.
